<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-parent</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Language Proxy Protocol</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-protocol</artifactId>

    <!--
        This module owns proxy.proto, its generated Java, and the hand-written classes that bridge a
        well-known type to a JVM type for every JVM speaker of the protocol (WireDurations,
        WireTimestamps - their javadoc and the README say why the bridges live here rather than in
        each caller), and the specification and client-authoring guide under docs/.

        NOTHING DEPENDS ON IT YET, and that is the point of landing it alone: the contract is
        reviewable as a wire contract before an engine or a client exists to argue about. When they
        arrive, the JVM sidecar and the Java gRPC transport depend on this module; the Java DIRECT
        transport must never depend on it, and non-JVM clients generate from
        src/main/proto/parallelconsumer/proxy/v1/proxy.proto by path with no Maven dependency at all.

        The schema is FROZEN (the language-proxy plan's freeze unit, astubbs#242): the wire may
        only gain, never change. The banner at the top of proxy.proto is the authority on what
        that means, and bin/check-proto-breaking.sh (buf breaking, FILE category) enforces it.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!--
            This module's real runtime floor is Java 17, and it must say so.

            The project-wide release.target is 8 (Jabel compiles Java 17 source to Java 8
            bytecode), but this module exists solely for the proxy family - the sidecar engine and
            the gRPC transport - whose floor is 17, because gRPC's Java runtime is. Publishing
            Java 8 bytecode here would claim a runtime floor the family never supports, and would
            route the entire generated message surface through Jabel for the benefit of no
            consumer. Nothing in the build can detect a wrong claim here: a release target of 8
            would compile, and the failure would arrive at a downstream consumer's runtime. Same
            shape as parallel-consumer-mutiny's override, whose pom carries the longer version.
        -->
        <release.target>17</release.target>

        <!--
            protobuf-java pairs with the gRPC version, and the generated code and the runtime
            must agree. ${grpc.version} is inherited from the root pom, which owns it for every
            module that speaks the protocol - see its comment for why it is not declared here.

            protobuf.version stays here, and deliberately: this module is its only consumer and it
            selects the protoc binary below. It is also read out of THIS pom BY PATH - by the
            protoc cache warm in .github/workflows/maven.yml today, and by the per-language protoc
            provisioning in .github/workflows/clients.yml once the foreign clients generate from
            the schema. Both readers hard-error on a renamed property rather than warming nothing,
            which is the only reason moving it would cost anything. Bump the pair together.

            3.25.8, NOT the 3.25.5 this module carried on astubbs#293, and the root pom's
            requireUpperBoundDeps is what decided it: grpc-protobuf ${grpc.version} asks for
            protobuf-java 3.25.8 transitively, so declaring 3.25.5 directly settles the graph on a
            version LOWER than something in it asks for - the exact shape that rule exists to catch.
            That rule postdates the branch this module was cut from, so this is a condition of
            landing on master rather than a defect there. Both halves move together, which is the
            point of one property: the protoc that compiles the schema is selected from it too, so
            generated code and runtime still agree.
        -->
        <protobuf.version>3.25.8</protobuf.version>

        <!--
            Which protoc compiles the schema. The default downloads the platform binary for
            exactly ${protobuf.version} from Maven Central, so generated code always matches the
            protobuf-java runtime. For an offline build, -Dproxy.protoc=PATH switches to a protoc
            found on the system PATH - at whatever version that binary happens to be, so the
            default is what CI and releases must use.

            src/test/scripts/verify-offline-protoc-parity.sh proves the two routes generate
            byte-identical output - run it after touching this wiring.
        -->
        <proxy.protoc>${protobuf.version}</proxy.protoc>
    </properties>

    <!--
        MODULE-LOCAL, and it must stay module-local. gRPC is the only thing in this repository that
        drags error_prone_annotations in twice at different versions - grpc-stub asks for 2.30.0
        directly while the guava it also brings asks for 2.47.0 - so resolution settles on 2.30.0 and
        the root pom's requireUpperBoundDeps fails the build. Managing it up to what the highest
        requester asks for is the fix that rule prescribes.

        NOT the root pom's ${error-prone.version}, which is a different artifact for a different job:
        that property pins the Error Prone COMPILER plugin, and it is pinned BELOW current on purpose
        (its own comment records that 2.43.0 is compiled to a class file version JDK 17 cannot load).
        Coupling this module's annotation version to it would mean this pin moves whenever that
        constraint lifts, for no reason connected to either. The annotations are CLASS-retention, so
        nothing reads them at runtime and no consumer inherits this.
    -->
    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.google.errorprone</groupId>
                <artifactId>error_prone_annotations</artifactId>
                <version>2.47.0</version>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.google.protobuf</groupId>
            <artifactId>protobuf-java</artifactId>
            <version>${protobuf.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.grpc</groupId>
            <artifactId>grpc-stub</artifactId>
            <version>${grpc.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.grpc</groupId>
            <artifactId>grpc-protobuf</artifactId>
            <version>${grpc.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!-- The generated ProxyServiceGrpc carries @javax.annotation.Generated, which the JDK
             stopped shipping at Java 9. This is grpc-java's own prescription for that (see its
             README's "Download" section): compile-time only, so provided scope - nothing needs it
             at runtime, and it must not leak onto consumers' classpaths. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.tomcat</groupId>
            <artifactId>annotations-api</artifactId>
            <version>6.0.53</version>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!-- Shared test conventions (TestConventionsArchTest) live in core's test-jar. This is
             test scope only: the production dependency arrow runs engine -> protocol, never
             protocol -> core. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
            <artifactId>parallel-consumer-core</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
            <classifier>tests</classifier>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!-- Declared explicitly rather than leaned on transitively: core's test-jar reaches for
             commons-lang3, and test scope is not transitive. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
            <artifactId>commons-lang3</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
                io.github.ascopes, not the long-standard org.xolstice plugin: xolstice was archived
                read-only in April 2025 (last release 2018), and needed the os-maven-plugin
                classifier dance to find a platform protoc. The ascopes plugin is maintained and
                resolves platform-correct protoc and protoc plugin binaries itself.

                3.10.3 is the newest line this build can run, not the newest release: every 4.x and
                5.x release declares a Maven 3.9.6 prerequisite ("versions before 3.9.6 cannot
                handle Java 17 bytecode in Eclipse Sisu") and the wrapper pins Maven 3.9.0, which
                fails the build with PluginIncompatibleException - measured here, not assumed. If
                the wrapper ever moves past 3.9.6, upgrade this plugin with it; the 4.x rename to
                watch is binaryMavenPlugins -> plugins kind="binary-maven" and protocVersion ->
                protoc.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>io.github.ascopes</groupId>
                <artifactId>protobuf-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.10.3</version>
                <configuration>
                    <protocVersion>${proxy.protoc}</protocVersion>
                    <!--
                        NOT the plugin's default of target/generated-sources/protobuf, and the
                        difference is load-bearing: the root pom's build-helper-maven-plugin adds
                        target/generated-sources as a TEST source root in every module, and a source
                        root sweeps its whole subtree - so anything generated beneath that path is
                        compiled twice, once as a main source (this plugin registers its output as a
                        main root) and again by testCompile into test-classes. Measured on this
                        module: the default path duplicated every generated class into
                        target/test-classes. Keeping the output outside generated-sources entirely
                        is what makes the generated code appear exactly once on the compile path.
                    -->
                    <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/generated-protobuf</outputDirectory>
                    <binaryMavenPlugins>
                        <binaryMavenPlugin>
                            <groupId>io.grpc</groupId>
                            <artifactId>protoc-gen-grpc-java</artifactId>
                            <version>${grpc.version}</version>
                        </binaryMavenPlugin>
                    </binaryMavenPlugins>
                </configuration>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>generate</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <!-- The root pom filters test resources (property substitution), and filtering a binary
                 corrupts it - or, as measured here, fails the build outright on a byte sequence that
                 is not valid UTF-8. The golden wire fixtures (golden-*.bin) are protobuf bytes whose
                 EXACT content is the point, so .bin is excluded from filtering rather than the
                 fixtures moved out of resources. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-resources-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <nonFilteredFileExtensions>
                        <nonFilteredFileExtension>bin</nonFilteredFileExtension>
                    </nonFilteredFileExtensions>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <!-- Generated code is not coverage-bearing, and jacoco's @Generated annotation filter
                 does not fire for protobuf gencode - the exclusion must be explicit. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <excludes>
                        <exclude>bz/stub/parallelconsumer/proxy/protocol/v1/**</exclude>
                    </excludes>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <!-- Same reason as the jacoco exclusion: generated code is not documentation-bearing. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <excludePackageNames>bz.stub.parallelconsumer.proxy.protocol.v1</excludePackageNames>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
